    Contextualized talk relevance to user's mathematical interests

    This is Tao's ICM 2026 public lecture, and its structure is telling: he deliberately sidesteps the capability debate. He conditions on a "Working Hypothesis" (AI will reasonably soon do a reasonable fraction of research math reasonably well) and asks the orthogonal question instead — what are the actual goals of the mathematical community, explicit and implicit?

    The core argument runs through Goodhart's law. Historically, math's many goals (solving problems, building theory, training people, creating aesthetic works) were correlated enough that one or two could proxy for the rest. AI-driven optimization decouples them. His case study iterates the goal "solve as many problems as possible" through five refinements — add verification, then exposition, then community acceptance, then canonicalization into the definitive theory of the field — and at each stage AI helps less. Canonicalization, the slowest and least AI-optimizable stage, is what he calls the most valuable. The punchline framing: math is moving from proof scarcity to proof abundance, and the bottleneck shifts to "proof digestion" — verification, refereeing, and consensus, work currently treated as low-prestige.

    A few concrete anchors: the First Proof challenge (7/10 novel research problems solved at publication quality in May 2026, $10–1000 per problem), Erdős problems accumulating AI proofs nobody has vouched for, and the Leiden Declaration recommendations — his own rule of thumb being that if authors can't give a clear expert-level talk on their result, it shouldn't be published.

    It maps almost exactly onto the metric-optimization vs. foundational-value framework you've been writing about — Tao is essentially arguing the math community needs to make its objective function explicit before an optimizer arrives that will exploit the gap between proxy and target. Also a nice touch: "All em-dashes in these slides were human-generated."

    Anything you want to dig into — the digestion bottleneck, the Goodhart framing, or how this compares to your own writing on the topic?

